BLOCK|h1|1|Chapter 28: Ultra-Token-Efficient Developer Ergonomics
BLOCK|p||When human developers and AI coding agents write software, verbose boilerplate wastes cognitive effort, consumes expensive model context tokens, and increases latency. Cluster-lang is designed with an **AI-First, Token-Efficient Ergonomics** philosophy: concise, expressive syntax that achieves maximum productivity with minimal characters.
BLOCK|p||This chapter covers:
BLOCK|p||1. **Dual Source Extensions (`.cl` and `.zk`)** with unified execution.
BLOCK|p||2. **String Interpolation (`${expr}`)** for readable, zero-boilerplate formatting.
BLOCK|p||3. **Collection & String Slicing (`s[start:end]`)** for fast sub-range access.
BLOCK|p||4. **Compact Model Instantiation (`Model(field=value)`)** eliminating multi-line setters.
BLOCK|p||5. **Elvis (`?:`) & Null-Coalescing (`??`) Operators** for safe fallback expressions.
BLOCK|p||6. **Pattern Matching (`match` / `case`)** for elegant branch dispatching.
BLOCK|p||7. **Compound Assignment Operators** (`+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, etc.).
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Dual Source Extensions: `.cl` and `.zk`
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang supports two official file extensions interchangeably:
BLOCK|list||`.cl` — The modern, compact branded extension (recommended for new projects).
BLOCK|list||`.zk` — The original zero-knowledge systems extension.
BLOCK|p||Both compile with identical performance, zero configuration, and direct execution:
BLOCK|code|bash|# Run directly with the unified cl driver
./cl run app.cl
./cl run app.zk

# Compile to a native stripped ELF binary
./cl build -O3 app.cl -o app
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. String Interpolation (`${expr}`)
BLOCK|p||Instead of chaining multiple string concatenations (`"Hello, " + name + "! Count: " + to_text(count)`), Cluster-lang provides native string interpolation using `${expr}` syntax:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    user := "Cluster Developer"
    version := 2
    tokens_saved := 42

    # Inline interpolation directly within string literals
    msg := "Welcome ${user}! Running Cluster v${version}. Economy: ${tokens_saved}%"
    put msg
BLOCK|p||Expressions inside `${}` are automatically evaluated and converted to text at compile-time with zero runtime overhead.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. String & Vector Slicing (`[start:end]`)
BLOCK|p||Extracting contiguous sub-slices of strings or vectors is as simple as Pythonic slicing:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    text := "Cluster-Language"

    # Slice from index 0 to 7
    prefix := text[0:7]       # "Cluster"
    
    # Slice from index 8 to length
    suffix := text[8:16]      # "Language"
    
    put "Prefix: " + prefix
    put "Suffix: " + suffix
BLOCK|p||Slicing performs safe boundary checks and produces isolated, memory-managed string slices.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Compact Model Instantiation
BLOCK|p||Instantiating models (structs) can be written as a single, clean expression with keyword assignments rather than multiple statements:
BLOCK|code|python|model HyperParameters:
    lr: float
    batch_size: int
    epochs: int
    optimizer: string

fn main():
    # Compact, one-line model construction
    cfg := HyperParameters(lr=0.001, batch_size=32, epochs=100, optimizer="adamw")

    put "Training with batch size: " + to_text(cfg.batch_size)
BLOCK|p||This syntax dramatically cuts down code tokens generated by AI coding assistants while remaining 100% type-safe.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|5. Elvis (`?:`) & Null-Coalescing (`??`)
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang provides the Elvis operator `?:` and Null-coalescing operator `??` for default fallbacks:
BLOCK|code|python|fn get_server_port(env_port: string) -> string:
    # If env_port is empty or null, fall back to "8080"
    return env_port ?: "8080"

fn resolve_timeout(timeout: int) -> int:
    # If timeout is 0 (falsy), use default 30
    return timeout ?: 30
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|6. Pattern Matching (`match` / `case`)
BLOCK|p||Replace long chains of `if / elif / else` with expressive pattern matching:
BLOCK|code|python|fn describe_http_status(code: int) -> string:
    match code:
        case 200:
            return "OK"
        case 400:
            return "Bad Request"
        case 404:
            return "Not Found"
        case 500:
            return "Internal Server Error"
        case _:
            return "Unknown Status"
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|7. Compound Assignment Operators
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang natively lowers arithmetic compound assignments directly to efficient single-instruction LLVM IR:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    score := 10
    score += 5     # 15
    score -= 3     # 12
    score *= 2     # 24
    score /= 4     # 6
    score %= 4     # 2
    put "Final score: " + to_text(score)
BLOCK|hr||
